## ABSTRACT
High-performance energy storage and sensing devices have been undergoing rapid development to meet the demand for portable and wearable electronic products, which require flexibility, extensibility, small volume and lightweight. In this study, we construct a lightweight and flexible self-powered sensing system by integrating a highly stretchable strain sensor with a high-performance asymmetric supercapacitor based on ZnSe/CoSe<sub>2</sub>//ECNT (ECNT: electrochemically activated carbon nanotube film). The ZnSe/CoSe<sub>2</sub> two-dimensional nanosheets on carbon nanotube (CNT) films are synthesized through a simple and efficient strategy derived from ZnCo-based metal-organic frameworks (MOFs). The density functional theory (DFT) simulations show the higher conductivity of the ZnSe/CoSe<sub>2</sub>/CNT electrode than the CoSe<sub>2</sub>/CNT electrode. Due to the synergistic properties of self-supported two-dimensional ZnSe/CoSe<sub>2</sub> nanosheets with high specific surface area and the high pathway of one-dimention CNTs, the nanocomposite electrode provides efficient transmission and short paths for electron/ion diffusion. The asymmetric supercapacitor provides a stable output power supply to the sensors that can precisely respond to strain and pressure changes. The sensor can also be attached to a garment for measuring a variety of joint movements.
## KEYWORDS
asymmetric supercapacitor, sensors, self-powered, self-supported ZnSe/CoSe<sub>2</sub>/carbon nanotube (CNT) nanosheets, density functional theory
## 1 Introduction
Many novel flexible and wearable supercapacitors (SCs) and sensors have been developed in recent years for applications in wearable electronics, artificial skin and disease diagnosis [1–3]. One of the key challenges for fabricating high-performance flexible SCs is manufacturing electrodes with similar specific capacitances as nonflexible SCs. Transition-metal selenides have been widely recognized as a highly promising contender due to their relatively low cost, rich redox chemistry and good structural stability besides their superior electrical conductivity and electrochemical activity [4, 5]. Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) are porous crystalline materials constructed by integrating metal ions or clusters with organic linkers. MOFs are promising precursors for the fabrication of target products including carbon materials, metal oxides, metal sulfides and metal selenides [6–8].
Flexibility is often a key requirement for electrodes intended for wearable applications. Carbon nanotube (CNT)-based SCs have shown not only high electrochemical performance but also lightweight and flexibility [9–14]. Flexible conductive CNT films have been widely used as current collectors in SCs. We have successfully fabricated flexible CNT@graphene@PANI nanowire film SCs with a capacitance up to 261.5 mF·cm<sup>-2</sup> [15], yarn asymmetric SCs (ASCs) from CNT@CuCo<sub>2</sub>O<sub>4</sub> and CNT@PPy electrodes (59.55 mF·cm<sup>-2</sup>) [16], and novel
reconfigurable and editable SCs based on MnO<sub>2</sub>/CNT with high energy density up to 27.14 Wh·kg<sup>-1</sup> [17].
Unlike conventional sensors which are usually nonflexible, CNTs and graphene can be integrated with elastomer substrates to achieve very high flexibility and stretchability for use in strain sensors and temperature sensors [18–21]. In these sensors, CNTs and graphene act as response materials to respond to changes in temperature, light and strain [22–24]. Such flexible strain sensors can be combined with flexible SCs as power source to constructed self-powered sensor systems [25].
Here we report the development of highly flexible self-powered sensor system consisting of a high sensitivity rGO-based flexible sensor laminated with an equally flexible and stretchable ZnSe/CoSe<sub>2</sub> film ASC. The integrated ASC can provide stable 0.8 V output to power the sensor. The unique sensor can precisely respond to strains up to 150%. The CNT@rGO//CNT self-powered sensor system is used as a wearable device to detect various human motions.
## 2 Experimental
## 2.1 Production of carbon nanotube film
CNT film was fabricated by CVD method where ethanol, ferrocene, and thiophene were used as carbon source, catalyst
precursor, and promoter, respectively. Briefly, the feedstock of ethanol containing \(1.2\;\mathrm{vol.}\%\) ferrocene and \(0.4\;\mathrm{vol.}\%\) thiophene, carried by \(\mathrm{Ar}/\mathrm{H}_{2}\), was injected at the rate of \(20\;\mathrm{mL\cdot h^{-1}}\) into a reactor at high temperature \((>1,100\;^{\circ}\mathrm{C})\). CNTs were synthesized and entangled in the hot zone forming a stocking-like aerogel. This thin CNT aerogel was blown out continuously from the reactor and collected by a wheel rotating perpendicularly to the gas flow, which formed macroscopic CNT films. This CNT films can be further densified by using volatile solutions to enhance their mechanical and physical properties.
## 2.2 Synthesis of CNT@ZnCo-MOF
Prior to synthesis, CNT films with area about \(9\;\mathrm{cm}^{2}\) were cleaned by acetone, deionized water and ethanol. After being dried, the CNT films were stuck to the inner wall of the beaker. In a typical synthesis of CNT@ZnCo-MOF, \(15\;\mathrm{mmol}\) 2-methylimidazole were dissolved in \(40\;\mathrm{mL}\) deionized water; \(20\;\mathrm{mmol}\) zinc nitrate hexahydrate and \(40\;\mathrm{mmol}\) cobalt nitrate hexahydrate were dissolved in \(20\;\mathrm{mL}\) deionized water. The two solutions were added into the 2-methylimidazole solution at the same time and subjected to magnetic stirring at room temperature for \(12\;\mathrm{h}\). After the reaction, the product was collected, washed with distilled water three times, and then thermally treated at \(300\;^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(2\;\mathrm{h}\) in air.
## 2.3 Synthesis of CNT@ZnSe/CoSe2
Typically, \(3\;\mathrm{g}\;\mathrm{NaOH}\), \(0.4\;\mathrm{g}\;\mathrm{Se}\) powder and \(35\;\mathrm{mL}\) of distilled water were transferred into a \(50\;\mathrm{mL}\) Teflon-lined stainless autoclave and the mixture was stirred for \(20\;\mathrm{min}\). The autoclave was sealed and heated to \(220\;^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) and maintained at the temperature for \(24\;\mathrm{h}\). After the autoclave was cooled to room temperature, the CNT@ZnCo-MOF was placed into the solution. The autoclave was sealed, heated to \(220\;^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) and maintained for \(12\;\mathrm{h}\). After the autoclave cooled down to room temperature, the converted sample was washed with distilled water and dried at \(50\;^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) under vacuum.
## 2.4 Fabrication of electrochemically activated carbon nanotube (ECNT) film
The pure CNT film was soaked in a mixed acid of \(\mathrm{HNO}_{3}\) and \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}\) (\(24\;\mathrm{mL}\), volume ratio from 2:1, 1:1, 1:2 to 1:3) for different times (2, 10, 20, \(30\;\mathrm{min}\)), then was washed with de-ionized water and dried at \(60\;^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\).
## 2.5 Synthesis of CNT@rGO
The as-prepared CNT films were cleaned by ethanol and then dried at \(60\;^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). rGO was purchased from XFNANO Inc. (XF-002-2). \(5\;\mathrm{mg}\;\mathrm{rGO}\) was dissolved in \(50\;\mathrm{mL}\) deionized water and strong sonication for \(30\;\mathrm{min}\). \(1.06\;\mathrm{g}\) lithium perchlorate was then dissolved in the above solution. The CNT@rGO film was obtained by electrochemical oxidation treatment carried out in a standard three electrode cell in a mixed solution with a platinum sheet as a counter electrode and \(\mathrm{Ag/AgCl}\) as a reference electrode at a constant voltage of \(-1.2\;\mathrm{V}\) for 1, 2, 4 h, as required. and the product was washed with distilled water and dried at \(60\;^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) under vacuum.
## 2.6 Preparation of the ZnSe/CoSe2/ECNT ASC
Polyvinyl alcohol (PVA)/LiCl gel electrolyte was prepared by mixing PVA \((3\;\mathrm{g})\) and LiCl \((5\;\mathrm{g})\) with deionized water \((28\;\mathrm{mL})\) and heated to \(95\;^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) under vigorous stirring until it became clear. The electrodes were coated with the PVA / LiCl and dried at \(60\;^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(1\;\mathrm{h}\), and then the PVA/LiCl coated \(\mathrm{ZnSe/CoSe}_{2}\) and ECNT electrodes were placed in parallel, followed by coating with PVA/LiCl again to produce a solid-state flexible film, which was used in the ASC device.
which was used in the ASC device.
## 2.7 Fabrication of strain sensor
PVA gel electrolyte was prepared by mixing PVA \((3\;\mathrm{g})\) with deionized water \((28\;\mathrm{mL})\) and heated to \(95\;^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) under vigorous stirring until it became clear. The CNT@rGO and pure CNT electrodes were coated with the PVA and dried at \(60\;^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(1\;\mathrm{h}\), and then the PVA coated CNT@rGO and pure CNT electrodes were placed in parallel, followed by coating with PVA again to produce a solid-state flexible film, which was used in the strain sensor device.
## 2.8 Fabrication of self-powered integrated sensor and ASC
A liquid mixture of polydimethylsiloxane (PDMS) base and a cruing agent (Sylgard 184, Silicone Elastomer, Dow Corning) was firstly mixed with a ratio of 10:1 and degassed under reduced pressure in a vacuum pump to remove bubble. Then, the liquid PDMS was heated on a hot plate at \(60\;^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for about \(30\;\mathrm{min}\) and the PDMS was not fully cured. The stretchable PDMS was first stretched, and then the ASC device was paced face-to-face onto the partially cured PDMS, subsequently, the sensor was coated on the opposite side of the PDMS. Between the ASC and sensor and PDMS, PVA gel was coated, after the gel became a solid, the pre-strain in the substrate was relaxed.
## 2.9 Characterization
X-ray diffraction (XRD, D8 Advance), Raman spectra (DXR2xi), and X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS, VG Multilab 2000) were used to characterize the structures. The microstructure was measured by scanning electron microscopy (SEM, Hitachi SU8010) and transmission electron microscopy (TEM, G220STWIN). The thermogravimetry (TGA) analysis (NETZSCH-TG209-F3) in air from 50 to \(700\;^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) at \(10\;^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\cdot\mathrm{min}^{-1}\). Cyclic voltammetry (CV), electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS), and galvanostatic charge-discharge (GCD) measurements were carried out on an electrochemical workstation (Ivium, CompactState 10 800).
## 2.10 Calculations
All calculations were based on density functional theory (DFT), using the Vienna ab initio simulation package (VASP) code [26]. The exchange and correlation terms were described using general gradient approximation (GGA) in the scheme of Perdew-Burke-Ernzerhof (PBE) [27]. Core electrons were described by pseudopotentials generated from the projector augmented wave method [28], and valence electrons are expanded in a plane-wave basis set with an energy cutoff of \(450\;\mathrm{eV}\). Since the Co atom is magnetic element, spin-polarized DFT calculations were performed. For the sampling of Brillouin-zone integrals, Gamma centered k-points grid of \(10\times8\times6\) and \(5\times4\times3\) were used for \(\mathrm{CoSe}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{ZnSe/CoSe}_{2}\). The convergence criterions of force and energy were set as \(0.01\;\mathrm{eV}\cdot\mathring{\mathrm{A}}^{-1}\) and \(10^{-4}\;\mathrm{eV}\), respectively.
## 3 Results and discussion
## 3.1 Strain and pressure sensing
Preparation of the rGO//CNT flexible strain sensor is detailed in the experimental section. Figure 1(a) shows schematically the ASC-powered flexible strain sensor. The conductivity of the CNT@rGO film is tested by a four-probe conductivity meter at a probe distance of \(1\;\mathrm{cm}\). Figure 1(b) shows that the rGO nanosheets are evenly dispersed on the CNT film. Figure 1(c)
presents the Raman spectra of the pure CNT film and the CNT@rGO films obtained with reaction time from 1 to \(4\;\mathrm{{h}}\). The D and G peaks from the rGO nanosheets in CNT@rGO are wider and stronger than those of the pure CNT film, indicating that the rGO nanosheets were successfully deposited on the CNT film. As the electrodeposition time increases, the intensity ratio between the D peak and the G peak \((I_{D}/I_{G})\) first increase and then decreases with a maximum value appearing at a reaction time of \(2\,\,\mathrm{h}\). With the electrodeposition time increases, the rGO content gradually increases, resulting in an increase in disorder and in the Raman signal of D band peak. The signal of G band which is associated with the \(\mathrm{sp}^{2}\)-hybridized carbon. But when the rGO started accumulating, the electrodeposition of rGO will transferred \(\mathrm{sp}^{3}\) bonded carbon atoms into \(\mathrm{sp}^{2}\) bonded carbon atoms, and then the increased the signal of G band. The \(I_{D}/I_{G}\) change is reflected by the change of electrical conductivity of the film in Fig. 1(d), showing the highest electrical conductivity at the \(2\,\mathrm{{h}}\) treatment time.
The sensor responds to both tensile strain and press. Figure 1(e) shows the gauge factor (GF) of the rGO@CNT sensor calculated from the equation
\[
\mathrm{GF}=(\Delta R/R_0)/(\Delta L/L_0)
\]
where \(L_{0}\) is the initial length of the sensor, and the \(\Delta L\) is the length change; \(R_{0}\) is the initial resistance of the sensor and \(\Delta R\) is the resistance change due to the applied strain. The high sensitivity \((\mathrm{GF}\,=\,4.42\) for \(0{-}80\%\) strain and \(\mathrm{GF}\,=\,9.65\) for \(80\%{-}150\%\) strain) and large sensing range of the sensor are attributed to the special rGO@CNT composite network structure. The response curve can be divided into two highly linear stages \(\mathrm{GF}_{1}\) and \(\mathrm{GF}_{2}\). In stage 1 (up to \(80\%\) strain), the relative resistance increases linearly with applied strain. The rGO@CNT conductive network exhibits elastic behaviors and excellent recovery after strain is released, and the sensor displaces highly repeatable result in cyclic testing (Fig. 1(f)).
The sensor shows a high GF value of 4.42 in this stage. When the strain is increased to above \(80\%\) (stage 2), the GF increases to 9.65, indicating a higher sensitivity of the rGO@CNT fiber/PVA film at large strains. This is because that in stage 2, the conductive composite film encounters increased relative movement between the conductive components, i.e., CNTs and graphene. Figure 1(g) shows the dynamic sensor responses at different tensile strains, \(80\%\) , \(100\%\) , \(120\%\) , and \(150\%\). The inset shows the sensor under \(0\%\) and \(150\%\) strain. When stepwise strain is applied, the sensor responds with a generally stable step-wise increase of resistance, albeit accompanied by a small overshoot due to stress relaxation in the conductive composite film (Fig. 1(h)).
## 3.2 Integrated ASC
The morphological features of the as-prepared samples were studied in detail using TEM, high-resolution HRTEM and selected-area electron diffraction (SAED). The XRD spectra of \( \mathrm{CNT@ZnCo-MOF} \) and \( \mathrm{CNT@ZnSe/CoSe}_{2} \) are provided in Fig. S2 in the ESM. In Fig. S2(a) in the ESM, the diffraction peaks at \( 19.07^{\circ} \), \( 31.47^{\circ} \), \( 36.96^{\circ} \), \( 38.78^{\circ} \), \( 45.07^{\circ} \), \( 55.66^{\circ} \), \( 59.69^{\circ} \), \( 65.19^{\circ} \), \( 67.86^{\circ} \), \( 68.99^{\circ} \), \( 73.99^{\circ} \) and \( 77.55^{\circ} \) collected from \( \mathrm{ZnCo-MOF} \) can be well indexed to the orthorhombic phase of \( \mathrm{ZnCo-MOF} \) (JCPDS card No.00-001-1149). In Fig. S2(b) in the ESM, the diffraction peaks at \( 45.31^{\circ} \), \( 53.89^{\circ} \) and \( 72.68^{\circ} \) can be well indexed to the orthorhombic phase of \( \mathrm{ZnSe} \) (JCPDS card No.00-001-0690), and the diffraction peaks at \( 28.97^{\circ} \), \( 30.77^{\circ} \), \( 34.53^{\circ} \), \( 35.96^{\circ} \), \( 43.96^{\circ} \), \( 47.73^{\circ} \), \( 50.23^{\circ} \), \( 53.49^{\circ} \), \( 56.95^{\circ} \), \( 63.29^{\circ} \) and \( 65.08^{\circ} \) can be well indexed to the orthorhombic phase of \( \mathrm{CoSe}_{2} \) (JCPDS card No.00-053-0449), the excess peaks are due to the selenium impurities (JCPDS card No.00-001-0848). As the \( \mathrm{ZnSe} \) is doped in \( \mathrm{CoSe}_{2} \), the JCPDS cards of the two materials can be analyzed in conjunction with the XRD results. Figure 3(a) displays a representative TEM image of nanosheets separated from the \( \mathrm{CNT@ZnSe/CoSe}_{2} \) film, showing a sheet width of approximately \( 20\;\mathrm{nm} \). The TEM image of \( \mathrm{ZnSe/CoSe}_{2} \) nanosheet shows that the nanosheets are evenly distributed. HRTEM of the nanosheets, as shown in Fig. 3(b), displays clear lattice fringes with an interplanar spacing of \( 0.15\;\mathrm{nm} \), which corresponds to the (230) planes of the \( \mathrm{ZnSe/CoSe}_{2} \) core. The bright and ordered diffraction spots in the SAED pattern in Fig. 3(c) confirm the single-crystalline nature of the \( \mathrm{ZnSe/CoSe}_{2} \) nanosheets. The element distribution of \( \mathrm{ZnSe/CoSe}_{2} \) nanosheets was shown in Fig. S3 in the ESM, where the \( \mathrm{Zn} \), \( \mathrm{Co} \), \( \mathrm{Se} \) were evenly distributed. In the positive electrode material, the nanostructural features of the \( \mathrm{ZnSe/CoSe}_{2} \) provide large electrode/electrolyte contact interfaces and this reduce the ion diffusion path for fast electrochemical kinetics.
The chemical bonding states of \( \mathrm{Zn} \), \( \mathrm{Co} \) and \( \mathrm{Se} \) on the surface of \( \mathrm{ZnSe/CoSe}_{2} \) were further evaluated by XPS (Figs. 3(d)–3(f)).
The binding energies of Se \( 3\mathrm{d}_{5/2} \) and \( 3\mathrm{d}_{3/2} \) at 55.3 and \( 56.1\;\mathrm{eV} \), respectively, are consistent with the reported data of Se in \( \mathrm{ZnSe/CoSe}_{2} \). Moreover, the peaks at \( 50.2\;\mathrm{eV} \) are attributed to selenium oxygen bonding at the surface. The electron-binding energies of Co \( 2\mathrm{p}_{3/2} \) at \( 779.3\;\mathrm{eV} \) and Co \( 2\mathrm{p}_{1/2} \) at \( 794.4\;\mathrm{eV} \) correspond to \( \mathrm{Co}^{2+} \) cations in \( \mathrm{ZnSe/CoSe}_{2} \). The TGA curves of the pure CNT film and \( \mathrm{ZnSe/CoSe}_{2} \) CNT film was shown in Fig. S4 in the ESM. Raman spectra of ECNTs show typical D \( (1,332\;\mathrm{cm}^{-1}) \) and G \( (1,585\;\mathrm{cm}^{-1}) \) peaks (Figs. 3(g) and 3(h)), where the G band represents the in-plane bond stretching motion of pairs of \( \mathrm{C}\;\mathrm{sp}^{2} \) atoms \( \mathrm{E}_{28} \) phonons), and the D band corresponds to the breathing modes of rings or Kpoint phonons of \( \mathrm{A}_{1\mathrm{g}} \) symmetry. After different concentrations of mixed acid treatments \( \left(V_{\mathrm{HNO}_{3}}\colon V_{\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}}\;=\;2{:}1\right. \) to 1:3), the intensity ratio of \( I_{\mathrm{D}}/I_{\mathrm{G}} \) increases from 0.672 to 0.964 and then decreases to 0.602. Acid treatment will graft active groups on the surface of CNTs, and the presence of active groups improves the D band signal. As the reaction time increases, the enhancement effect decreases, and the mixed acid will begin to destroy the structure of the material, resulting in a decrease in the D band signal. The XPS of ECNT can be found in Fig. S5 in the ESM.
Density functional theory (DFT) calculations were performed to compare \( \mathrm{CoSe}_{2} \) and \( \mathrm{ZnSe/CoSe}_{2} \). Based on the orthorhombic \( \mathrm{ZnSe} \) and \( \mathrm{CoSe}_{2} \), optimized crystal structures of \( \mathrm{CoSe}_{2} \) and \( \mathrm{ZnSe/CoSe}_{2} \) are built, and hence, density of states (DOS) calculations are performed to compare their electronic properties, which are shown in Figs. 4(a)–4(d). Notably, from Fig. 4(c), the band gap of the total DOS for \( \mathrm{ZnSe/CoSe}_{2} \) disappeared, and a high density of states is observed on \( E_{6} \), which clearly shows that \( \mathrm{ZnSe/CoSe}_{2} \) demonstrates obvious metallic characteristics with improved electron mobility and conductivity compared to \( \mathrm{CoSe}_{2} \). To appraise the electrochemical performance of the samples, the as-fabricated electrodes were first investigated in a three-electrode system with a \( 3\;\mathrm{M} \) LiCl electrolyte. The higher CV integrated area \( (100\;\mathrm{mV}\cdot\mathrm{s}^{-1}) \) and lower internal
resistance indicated that the ZnSe/CoSe\(_2\) electrode had higher electrochemical properties than CoSe\(_2\) (Figs. 4(e) and 4(f)). The EIS data can be fitted by using an equivalent circuit consisting of a bulk solution resistance, \(R_{\mathrm{s}}\), a charge-transfer resistance, \(R_{\mathrm{ct}}\), a pseudocapacitive element, \(C_{\mathrm{p}}\), and a constant-phase element (CPE) accounting for the double-layer capacitance, as shown in the inset of Fig. 4(f). The \(R_{\mathrm{s}}\) and \(R_{\mathrm{ct}}\) values can be obtained from the Nyquist plots, in which the high-frequency semicircle intercepts the real axis at \(R_{\mathrm{s}}\) and \(R_{\mathrm{s}}+R_{\mathrm{ct}}\). The electrochemical properties of ZnSe/CoSe\(_2\) and ECNT are shown in Fig. 4(g) and Fig. S6(a) in the ESM. The CV curves of the ZnSe/CoSe\(_2\) electrode in the potential range of \(0{-}1.0\ \mathrm{V}\) exhibit
as shown in the inset of Fig. 4(f). The \(R_{\mathrm{s}}\) and \(R_{\mathrm{ct}}\) values can be obtained from the Nyquist plots, in which the high-frequency semicircle intercepts the real axis at \(R_{\mathrm{s}}\) and \(R_{\mathrm{s}}\!+\!R_{\mathrm{ct}}\) . The electrochemical properties of \(Z\mathrm{nSe/CoSe}_{2}\) and ECNT are shown in Fig. \(4(\mathrm{g})\) and Fig. S6(a) in the ESM. The CV curves of the \(Z\mathrm{nSe/CoSe}_{2}\) electrode in the potential range of \(0{-}1.0\;\mathrm{V}\) exhibit
typical pseudocapacitive behaviors with two pairs of redox peaks, indicating that the main capacitance is contributed by the redox reaction. Using Dunn's method, the total charge storage of \( \mathrm{ZnSe/CoSe}_{2} \) and ECNT is divided into the following two parts [30–31]: diffusion-controlled contribution and surface capacitive contribution, which improve the energy density and power density, respectively. In Fig. 4(h) and Fig. S6(b) in the ESM, the purple-shaded regions represent the contribution from the surface capacitance of the two materials at \( 1\ \mathrm{mV{\cdot}s^{-1}} \). Figure 4(i) and Fig. S4(c) in the ESM show the histograms for comparison of the capacity ratio of diffusion-controlled and surface capacitive charges for \( \mathrm{ZnSe/CoSe}_{2} \) nanosheets at \( 0.5{-}3\;\mathrm{mV}{\cdot}\mathrm{s}^{-1} \) and ECNT at \( 0.3{-}1\;\mathrm{mV}{\cdot}\mathrm{s}^{-1} \), respectively. The results demonstrate that the effective surface charge storage of \( \mathrm{ZnSe/CoSe}_{2} \) increased with increasing scan rate, and that the charge storage for ECNT largely depended on the diffusion contribution.
The detailed electrochemical properties of the resulting ECNT electrodes are provided in Figs. S7 and S8 in ESM. With the increase of \( \mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4} \) in the mixed acid solution, the pseudocapacitance gradually increases until achieving the highest capacitance at the \( \mathrm{HNO}_{3}{\mathrm{:}}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4} \) ratio of 1:2. The electrochemical results are consistent with the Raman results in Fig. 3. At fixed ratio of \( \mathrm{HNO}_{3}{\mathrm{:}}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4} \) of 1:2, the reaction duration was varied from 2 to \( 30\ \mathrm{min} \). The \( I_{\mathrm{D}}/I_{\mathrm{G}} \) ratio, which may be taken as an indicator of the oxygen functions on the surface of ECNT, increased from 0.576 to 0.845 and then decreased to 0.507. Figure S9 in the ESM shows that the ECNT electrode obtained the optimal results at the \( \mathrm{HNO}_{3}{\mathrm{:}}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4} \) ratio of 1:2 and the reaction time of \( 20\;\mathrm{min} \).
An ASC was assembled from the optimized \( \mathrm{ZnSe/CoSe}_{2} \) (positive electrode) and ECNT (negative electrode). To maximize the performance of the ASC device, the charges generated on the positive and negative electrodes should be balanced. The area ratio between the \( \mathrm{ZnSe/CoSe}_{2} \) and ECNT electrodes is calculated to be approximately 1:1 (Fig. S10(a) in ESM). The
electrochemical performance of the ASC was characterized by CV and galvanostatic charge/discharge (GCD) tests. Figure S10(b) in the ESM presents the CV curves of the optimized ASC device collected at different potential windows (between 0.8 and \( 1.6\;\mathrm{V} \)) at a constant scan rate of \( 30\;\mathrm{mV}{\cdot}\mathrm{s}^{-1} \), indicating that the potential window can be extended to \( 1.6\;\mathrm{V} \). The CV curves retained their quasi-rectangular shapes when the scan rate was increased from 10 to \( 200\ \mathrm{mV{\cdot}s^{-1}} \) (Fig. 5(a)), exhibiting pseudocapacitance and electric double-layer capacitance of the device. The curves have good symmetrical shapes even at a high scan rate of \( 200\;\mathrm{mV}{\cdot}\mathrm{s}^{-1} \), indicating that the ASC device has good rate capability and outstanding capacitive performance. The GCD curves maintained their approximate triangular shapes when the current was increased from 0.5 to \( 1.5\ \mathrm{mA} \) (Fig. S10(c) in the ESM). The CV and GCD profiles indicate excellent reversible charging and discharging capacitive properties of the ASC device. Figure 5(b) shows the capacitive retention rate of devices under different current densities. The volumetric capacitance of our ASC calculated from the result is \( 1.65\,\mathrm{F{\cdot}c m}^{-3} \) at the current density of \( 100\;\mathrm{mA{\cdot}c m}^{-3} \). The excellent electrochemical performance of the ASC is attributed mainly to the unique nanosheets structure of the \( \mathrm{ZnSe/CoSe}_{2} \).
The mechanical flexibility of our ASC device has also been investigated. Its electrochemical performance was evaluated under different bending angles (Fig. 5(c)) at a current density of \( 50\ \mathrm{mV{\cdot}s^{-1}} \). The photos of the device under different states were shown in Fig. S11 in the ESM. The capacitance retention was approximately \( 95.3\% \) after being bent by \( 180^{\circ} \) (Fig. 5(d)), demonstrating its excellent flexibility and electrochemical stability. As shown in the Fig. 5(e), the \( \mathrm{ZnSe/CoSe}_{2}//\mathrm{ECNT} \) ASC was strained from 0 to \( 150\% \), and the curves are almost unchanged. When the ASC device was strain from 0 to \( 80\% \) and cycled for 1,000 cycles, the capacitance was kept \( 87.4\% \) (Fig. 5(f)). The cycling durability of the \( \mathrm{ZnSe/CoSe}_{2}//\mathrm{ECNT} \) ASC was tested at \( 100\ \mathrm{mA{\cdot}c m}^{-3} \) up to 5,000 cycles (Fig. 5(g)),
at which the device retained \(80.1\%\) of its initial capacitance. This excellent stability is comparable with those of previously reported ASCs, such as \(\mathrm{MoO}_{3}\) nanobelt SCs \((77.4\%\) 5,000 cycles) [32]. Energy density and power density are two important parameters for the device evaluation. The ASC exhibited an energy density as high as \(1.87\mathrm{\mWh{\cdot}c m^{-3}}\) at a power density of \(320\mathrm{\mW{\cdot}c m^{-3}}\) and an energy density of \(0.67\mathrm{\mWh{\cdot}c m^{-3}}\) at a high power density of \(960\;\mathrm{mW}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-3}\) (Fig. 5(h)), which are much higher than those of many recently reported parallel ASCs, such as \(\mathrm{RGO//MoO_{3}}\) \((0.46\mathrm{\mWh{\cdot}c m^{-3}}\) at \(120\mathrm{\mW{\cdot}c m^{-3}}\) ) [33], \(\mathrm{MnO}_{2}//\mathrm{PEDOT:PSS}\) \((0.18\mathrm{\mWh{\cdot}c m^{-3}}\) at \(18\mathrm{\mW{\cdot}c m^{-3}}\) ) [34], \(\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{T}_{x}\) -based SCs \((0.15\mathrm{\mWh{\cdot}c m^{-3}}\) at \(0.15\mathrm{\mW{\cdot}c m^{-3}}\) ) [35], NCO//SiNWs \((1.3\ \mathrm{\mWh{\cdot}c m^{-3}}\) at \(10\;\mathrm{\mW{\cdot}c m^{-3}}\) ) [36], R\(\mathrm{PANI@PVA@DACNT}\) SCs \((0.572\,\mathrm{\mWh{\cdot}c m^{-3}}\) at \(74\,\mathrm{\mW{\cdot}c m^{-3}}\) ) [37] and PEDOT cm-Ni \((73.73\mathrm{\mWh@DACNT}\) SCs \(28\;\mathrm{cm}^{-3}\) ) [38]. The high energy density can be ascribed to the intrinsic high capacitance of \(\mathrm{ZnSe/CoSe}_{2}\) and the wide voltage window of the unique ASC architecture. The ASC Higher working voltage or current can be achieved by connecting two or more devices in series or parallel. Figure 5(i) shows the GCD curves of single ASC and ASCs connected in series and in parallel. The parallel-connected ASCs show a double voltage window of the single ASC, and the series-connected ASCs exhibit twice the capacitance of the single SC.
The ASC film (discussed later) and the sensor were laminated on the opposite sides of an elastic PDMS substrate to form a self-powered sensor system. The integrated sensing device was then attached to the finger, wrist and elbow as shown in Fig. 6(a) using an adhesive tape to monitor body motion. The ASC was charged to \(0.8\,\mathrm{~V~}\) to power the strain sensor. Figures 6(b)–6(d) shows the sensing test results of repeated bending and straightening of the joints. The electrical signals recorded the positions of the joints and therefore can be used for recognition of body gestures. Figure 6(e) shows that the sensing device can also be used as a pressure sensor responding to the tapping and pressing actions of a finger, which may be
used to represent the dot (lasting about 0.3 s) and dash (1 s) in telegraphic Morse code. Signals for the same letter are separated by \(2\;s,\) and signals between letters are separated by \(3\;s.\) as an example, Figure 6(f) shows the Morse code for "SCUEC". All these results demonstrate stable sensing performance of the integrated devices under dynamic tensile and compression strains, and can used in a variety of situations, such as recognition of body motions and pressure pulses with high sensitivity and repeatability. The sensor is integrated with an ASC based on \(\mathrm{ZnSe/CoSe}_{2}\) and ECNT electrodes to power the sensor. The construction and performance of the ASC is discussed below.
## 4 Conclusions
In this study, we fabricated a self-powered sensing system consisting of a \(\mathrm{ZnSe/CoSe}_{2}\) and ECNT-based ASC and a CNT@rGO-based strain sensor. The CNT@rGO sensor showed high sensitivity and a large sensing range up to \(150\%\) strain. The sensor has been demonstrated for measuring joint movements of finger, wrist and elbow as well as being used as a pressure sensor. The \(\mathrm{ZnSe/CoSe}_{2}\) was in situ grown on a CNT film, which was combined with an ECNT negative electrode to form an ASC. The ASC displayed very high capacitance performance of \(1.65\,\mathrm{\,F{\cdot}c m^{-3}}\) at the current density of \(64\mathrm{\mA{\cdot}c m^{-3}}\) with a stable output voltage of \(1.6\;\mathrm{V},\) allowing it to maintain high energy density \((1.87\mathrm{\mW{\cdot}h{\cdot}c m^{-3}}\) at a power density of \(320\mathrm{\mW{\cdot}c m^{-3}}\) ) and excellent cycle performance ( \(82\%\) discharge retention after 5,000 cycles). Electrodeposition of rGO nanosheets as conductive nanofillers onto a CNT film retained the excellent mechanical properties of CNT. The material conductivity can be tuned by adjusting the electrodeposition time. The optimized density of rGO contributed to the current conduction and electrolyte ion diffusion. These encouraging results show that the self-powered sensors can be used in mobile wearable devices in the next generation of high-performance flexible and lightweight mobile monitoring devices.
